1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,760
It was one of the most feared high-performance weapons of the 20th century when the MiG-15 appeared in the skies over Korea.

2
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:11,640
It sent the unsuspecting West spiraling into a state of shock.

3
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:17,780
Until the arrival of the American F-86 Sabre, the Russian jet outclassed everything in the sky.

4
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, the MiG-15 on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:47,657 --> 00:00:52,080
Russia in the 1920s was a country of turmoil, revolution and change.

6
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:58,140
Stalin's reign of terror permeated every aspect of life in the Soviet Union.

7
00:00:58,141 --> 00:01:03,720
Soviet aircraft designers lived in a world of secrecy and fear.

8
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:10,800
Although well behind the West, Soviet designers were actively experimenting with advanced technology.

9
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:17,600
But by the late 1930s, much of this innovative work would never see the light of day.

10
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:27,740
Aircraft designers knew that to fail could mean banishment to a labor camp in Siberia or a bullet in the back of the head.

11
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:39,920
You're living under an administration which was happy to shoot large numbers of engineers or anybody else on simple whims, political whims or whatever.

12
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:45,380
And so there was an atmosphere that really is very difficult for people in the West to understand.

13
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:54,420
Stalin astonished the world in August 1939 by signing a non-aggression pact with Germany.

14
00:01:54,421 --> 00:02:04,420
When his top engineer discovered that the German ME-109 fighter was far superior to anything in the Soviet Union, Stalin flew into a rage

15
00:02:04,421 --> 00:02:11,840
Nikolai Polykarpov, his chief designer, immediately fell out of favor as too old and stayed

16
00:02:11,841 --> 00:02:15,840
Stalin began to search for a younger and more dynamic team

17
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:24,060
He selected a young designer named Artun Mikoyan to head a new design bureau

18
00:02:24,061 --> 00:02:26,700
and tapped Mikhail Gurevich as his deputy.

19
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:33,920
Artem Ivanovich was, of course, a designer,

20
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:36,740
but an exceptional organizer, too.

21
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:40,380
He was the ideas man,

22
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:45,220
whereas Gurevich was someone who was very good at detailing

23
00:02:45,221 --> 00:02:47,140
and fine-tuning the designs.

24
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,700
Artem Mikoyan was from Armenia, the son of a carpenter.

25
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:57,220
He had trained as a pilot in the Soviet Air Force and later became an engineer.

26
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,320
His brother was on Stalin's Council of Ministers.

27
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:07,240
By 1937, he had joined the main Soviet design bureau led by Polykarpov.

28
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,360
Here he worked on several successful aircraft designs.

29
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:16,940
Now he would replace his boss and lead the Soviet Union into the jet age.

30
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:21,400
Mikhail Goryevich was a Ukrainian.

31
00:03:21,401 --> 00:03:27,040
He trained as an engineer and after the revolution became an aircraft designer in Moscow.

32
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:30,360
Here he met Mikoyan and they became good friends.

33
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:34,900
Gurevich liked Artun Mikoyan a lot.

34
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:37,680
He was perfectly happy to be his deputy.

35
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:41,040
They were both kind and decent people.

36
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:43,800
And that's also why they worked so well together.

37
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:50,120
The model of the new experimental bureau was speed and altitude.

38
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:56,940
All their future aircraft designs would be known as MiGs, after the combination of its creators' names.

39
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:03,480
It was the start of a partnership that would be associated around the world with successful aircraft for decades.

40
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:15,640
Then, in June 1941, Hitler launched his surprise offensive against the Soviet Union.

41
00:04:15,641 --> 00:04:23,540
Stalin's air force was totally unprepared and ill-equipped to fight the modern German fighters.

42
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:29,380
Russian losses were massive.

43
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:41,540
Final victory over the Nazi war machine was largely due to the remarkable success of the Red Army.

44
00:04:41,541 --> 00:04:44,720
The Soviet air force was left in the dark ages.

45
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:55,740
By the end of the war, tensions that would soon turn into the Cold War began to grow.

46
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:00,720
The Allies now had the jet engine, space rockets, and the atom bomb.

47
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:07,180
Soviet designers needed to catch up, and catch up fast.

48
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:13,800
Stalin was furious that the Soviet Union's technology had fallen behind that of the West.

49
00:05:13,801 --> 00:05:16,560
and he told his designers to bridge the gap.

50
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:19,140
Not an easy task

51
00:05:19,141 --> 00:05:22,540
and Stalin wasn't the sort of man that you argued with

52
00:05:22,541 --> 00:05:26,220
but in one respect, Soviet technicians did have an advantage.

53
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:28,180
They captured a lot of German technology

54
00:05:28,181 --> 00:05:31,100
amongst it, the German Me 262 jet fighter

55
00:05:31,101 --> 00:05:33,360
and they were able to build on this

56
00:05:33,361 --> 00:05:35,140
to build their own jet

57
00:05:35,141 --> 00:05:37,740
at what was the beginning of the atomic age.

58
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:41,620
The Mikoyan and Gorjevich team

59
00:05:41,621 --> 00:05:43,540
took advantage of this captured technology

60
00:05:43,541 --> 00:05:46,920
and began to develop a new jet fighter, the MiG-9.

61
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:57,580
At first it was planned with MiG-9 to put the engines in the same place as the Me262.

62
00:05:58,940 --> 00:06:01,140
But later on a different decision was made,

63
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:03,560
and the engines were placed in the fuselage,

64
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:05,900
which was recognized to be more effective.

65
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,100
Tests proved that this was the correct decision.

66
00:06:09,101 --> 00:06:16,640
In April 1946, fitted with two German BMW jet engines,

67
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:21,140
the MiG-9 was the Soviet Union's first prototype jet ever to fly.

68
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:28,120
At high speeds, bailing out would now become sheer suicide,

69
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:32,540
so the first ejection seat in the USSR was successfully tested.

70
00:06:32,541 --> 00:06:39,400
It was just one of the jet age's many design problems that had to be solved.

71
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:46,280
They realized in order to reach high speed, close to the speed of sound,

72
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:48,800
they needed a new arrow-shaped wing.

73
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:52,380
That wing would push back the shock wave and air resistance

74
00:06:52,381 --> 00:06:54,800
in order to achieve much higher speeds.

75
00:06:54,801 --> 00:07:02,840
The design and transition from a straight wing to a swept back wing

76
00:07:02,841 --> 00:07:05,540
was to give the MiG its most famous characteristic.

77
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:10,380
Now with a cigar-shaped fuselage and swept wings,

78
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:13,860
all the jet fighter needed was a much more powerful engine.

79
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:20,700
The problem with the ME-262 engine, which the Germans had run into

80
00:07:20,701 --> 00:07:25,260
and was the major factor in its late appearance in the war

81
00:07:25,261 --> 00:07:29,760
was how do you make a turbine blade stay on the turbine

82
00:07:29,761 --> 00:07:32,860
given the kinds of pressures you're putting on a jet engine.

83
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:37,400
And that was a problem which the Soviets were not capable of solving.

84
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:42,180
At this crucial moment, the new post-war British government,

85
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:43,620
as part of a trade agreement,

86
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:46,300
invited Mikoyan and a Soviet delegation

87
00:07:46,301 --> 00:07:48,920
to visit the British Rolls-Royce engine factory.

88
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:56,780
For the Russians, it was a potential gold mine, access to the latest Rolls-Royce jet technology.

89
00:07:58,420 --> 00:08:03,040
Stalin reacted with typical suspicion, asking, what fool would sell us his secrets?

90
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:12,460
On arrival in July 1946, Mikoyan and the Soviet delegation were welcomed with typical British hospitality.

91
00:08:12,461 --> 00:08:13,161
day.

92
00:08:13,162 --> 00:08:20,620
He was invited to the house of one of the Rolls-Royce bosses, and they played English

93
00:08:20,621 --> 00:08:23,194
billiards. Atum played billiards well.

94
00:08:23,195 --> 00:08:26,920
I know, because I played billiards with him many times.

95
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:38,560
He'd lost one game, and then offered a challenge, and said, let's play another game,

96
00:08:38,561 --> 00:08:41,820
And if I win, you sell us your jet engines.

97
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:47,820
It was a close game, but the Russians won.

98
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:54,260
Bizarrely, the future course of the MiG was settled across the green felt of an English pool table.

99
00:08:58,660 --> 00:09:01,260
Mikoyan had beaten the British at their own game.

100
00:09:01,660 --> 00:09:05,040
He had won the famous Rolls-Royce jet engine on a bet.

101
00:09:05,041 --> 00:09:09,620
it sounds like an anecdote

102
00:09:09,621 --> 00:09:11,640
but he told me that this was true

103
00:09:11,641 --> 00:09:16,980
although the Russians were about to receive

104
00:09:16,981 --> 00:09:18,780
the best of British jet technology

105
00:09:18,781 --> 00:09:20,560
certain questions remained

106
00:09:20,561 --> 00:09:23,440
the visit to the Rolls Royce factory

107
00:09:23,441 --> 00:09:24,720
would put them to rest

108
00:09:24,721 --> 00:09:27,540
the Russians had another trick

109
00:09:27,541 --> 00:09:28,380
up their sleeves

110
00:09:28,381 --> 00:09:34,120
the most important question

111
00:09:34,121 --> 00:09:36,020
was what were the blades made of.

112
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:42,300
During the tour of the factory,

113
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:44,220
one of the members of the delegation

114
00:09:44,221 --> 00:09:47,020
wore special boots with spongy soles,

115
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:50,620
and he secretly trod all around the metal lathe,

116
00:09:50,780 --> 00:09:53,180
so lots of shavings stuck to his boots.

117
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:58,820
He took these shavings back to analyze in the USSR,

118
00:09:58,821 --> 00:10:01,420
so by the time the engine was purchased,

119
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:03,940
they already knew the composition of the alloy,

120
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:09,720
And this helped to speed up the copying of the Neen engine and launch the Russian engines into mass production.

121
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:19,540
The supposedly harmless gift of the jet engine turned out to be a deadly mistake by the British.

122
00:10:19,541 --> 00:10:33,260
the rolls-royce jet engine was quickly copied and stalin at last had his new generation of jet

123
00:10:33,261 --> 00:10:45,200
fighter aircraft in just four years mig-15s would be shooting down american and british

124
00:10:45,201 --> 00:10:46,620
planes over Korea.

125
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:49,780
The F-80C

126
00:10:49,781 --> 00:10:51,560
shooting star was America's

127
00:10:51,561 --> 00:10:53,120
first operational jet fighter.

128
00:10:53,500 --> 00:10:55,020
It was designed in 1943

129
00:10:55,021 --> 00:10:57,060
and first flew in January

130
00:10:57,061 --> 00:10:59,340
1944. Modern Marvels

131
00:10:59,341 --> 00:11:00,320
will return the moment.

132
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:03,380
You're watching MiG-15

133
00:11:03,381 --> 00:11:04,800
on Modern Marvels.

134
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:12,040
The Tuchina Airshow,

135
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:14,180
Moscow, July 1949.

136
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:22,080
A new generation of Soviet MiG-15 jet fighters are paraded in front of Stalin and the public for the first time.

137
00:11:23,580 --> 00:11:29,440
The MiG designers Mikoyan and Gorjevich had been awarded the Stalin Prize for their winning design.

138
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:38,200
The MiG-15 had a maximum speed of 652 miles per hour and was capable of flying at 50,000 feet.

139
00:11:38,201 --> 00:11:43,440
It was given powerful armaments

140
00:11:43,441 --> 00:11:47,580
with two 23mm and a single 37mm cannon

141
00:11:47,581 --> 00:11:51,080
Most of the pilots who flew the MiG-15

142
00:11:51,081 --> 00:11:52,820
were enormously impressed

143
00:11:52,821 --> 00:12:00,700
My first impressions are hard to describe in words

144
00:12:00,701 --> 00:12:04,660
In the MiG-15 the conditions for the pilot were wonderful

145
00:12:04,661 --> 00:12:06,700
It was designed with love

146
00:12:06,701 --> 00:12:14,180
The view was fantastic because the cockpit was right at the front of the plane.

147
00:12:14,820 --> 00:12:18,140
While I was flying, it was like gliding over a precipice.

148
00:12:26,420 --> 00:12:30,780
The MiG-15 at first sight seemed to me to be like a new day in aviation.

149
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:34,020
I can't describe it in words.

150
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:36,460
It was indescribable.

151
00:12:36,700 --> 00:12:42,031
First of all, silence. As if the plane was standing still on Earth.

152
00:12:42,032 --> 00:12:43,940
No vibrations, no noise.

153
00:12:46,820 --> 00:12:51,473
The speed's going up. 600, 700, 800. You feel overwhelmed.

154
00:12:51,474 --> 00:12:54,120
You just want to break into song.

155
00:12:54,121 --> 00:12:57,660
and even I want to sing.

156
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:09,340
But the Russians were still behind American technology

157
00:13:09,341 --> 00:13:12,540
in the race for the next generation of modern jet fighters.

158
00:13:15,300 --> 00:13:17,340
In the spring of 1949,

159
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:20,760
a U.S. Air Force F-86 Sabre jet

160
00:13:20,761 --> 00:13:23,200
had flown supersonic for the first time.

161
00:13:24,120 --> 00:13:29,620
The F-86, frankly, stood out in this field because of its graceful beauty

162
00:13:29,621 --> 00:13:33,600
and the fact that it was an airplane now with a jet engine,

163
00:13:34,200 --> 00:13:36,140
which we thought would be very simple,

164
00:13:36,860 --> 00:13:40,440
like turning a valve on to go and turning it off when you wanted to stop.

165
00:13:41,100 --> 00:13:42,400
It wasn't quite that simple.

166
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,640
But nevertheless, it was a fantastic advancement in aerospace.

167
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:51,500
Within a year of these outstanding developments,

168
00:13:51,501 --> 00:13:58,460
the American F-86 pilots would be locked in bitter combat with Russian MiG-15s over the skies of Asia.

169
00:13:59,780 --> 00:14:04,160
For the pilots of both countries, flying in the jet age would require new skills.

170
00:14:04,820 --> 00:14:09,780
Russian test pilots were worried about the tendency of the MiG to go into uncontrollable spins.

171
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:14,480
One of its test pilots was killed after putting the MiG into a spin.

172
00:14:14,481 --> 00:14:22,660
I have tested 22 types of planes for spinning characteristics.

173
00:14:23,600 --> 00:14:25,800
A spin has always been a difficult element of flying.

174
00:14:26,520 --> 00:14:30,460
In all planes, there were incidents when a pilot could not get out of a spin.

175
00:14:31,140 --> 00:14:34,620
When you are spinning, you have to switch off all your normal reflexes.

176
00:14:36,580 --> 00:14:39,480
Spinning out of control is every pilot's nightmare.

177
00:14:39,481 --> 00:14:46,480
To survive in the jet age, the MiG pilots needed to keep a cool head and handle the aircraft with great sensitivity.

178
00:14:53,020 --> 00:14:56,000
You have to overcome your instinctive responses.

179
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,260
This is what made the MiG so different from all previous planes.

180
00:15:00,261 --> 00:15:14,261
On May Day 1950, the MiG-15 flew past an unsuspecting audience in Moscow's Red Square in full view of Western diplomats.

181
00:15:16,260 --> 00:15:24,380
The intelligence failure of the Western observers to recognize Stalin's new powerful weapon in the skies above would have disastrous consequences.

182
00:15:30,260 --> 00:15:34,800
MiGs were already in the throes of full-scale production.

183
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:44,740
Only a month later, the Soviet-supported North Korean People's Army launched a massive invasion of South Korea.

184
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:57,300
Stalin thought that the communists could invade South Korea without generating any military response from the West.

185
00:15:57,301 --> 00:16:01,220
Yes, there'd be the usual diplomatic protests, but it wouldn't go any further than that.

186
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:05,360
In fact, he'd misjudged the situation.

187
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:08,220
President Truman responded quickly.

188
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,040
The United Nations agreed to send troops.

189
00:16:11,700 --> 00:16:17,680
Within four months, the invading North Koreans had been rolled right the way back.

190
00:16:19,200 --> 00:16:24,740
Now, American B-29 bombers roamed across North Korea's skies with no resistance,

191
00:16:24,741 --> 00:16:28,560
and fighter bombers continuously attacked troop movements at will.

192
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:37,280
By November 1950, UN forces had advanced right across North Korea

193
00:16:37,281 --> 00:16:40,380
and were approaching the Chinese border along the Yalu River.

194
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:51,480
Mao Zedong, the Chinese communist leader, needed a cause to unite his young nation.

195
00:16:52,200 --> 00:16:55,720
the American advance to China's border provided the perfect opportunity.

196
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:06,960
On Thanksgiving Day 1950, advanced units of the UN force in North Korea paused along the Yalu River, the border with China.

197
00:17:07,360 --> 00:17:15,900
On the following day, tens of thousands of soldiers of the Chinese People's Army stormed out of the mountains and attacked the UN forces.

198
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:31,320
As the bitter winter weather closed in, UN troops were forced into a humiliating retreat.

199
00:17:35,480 --> 00:17:40,320
Stalin knew he had to support his communist ally, but only under total secrecy.

200
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:50,340
In December 1950, Soviet MiGs were deployed in China to be flown secretly by disguised and highly experienced Russian pilots.

201
00:17:51,480 --> 00:18:03,260
Our departure to China was to remain a secret,

202
00:18:03,900 --> 00:18:06,140
so all our insignia had to be removed.

203
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:09,240
We looked as though we had been demobilized.

204
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:15,120
On arrival, we were all given Chinese uniforms,

205
00:18:15,740 --> 00:18:17,800
but most officers did not like it.

206
00:18:17,801 --> 00:18:25,280
So as soon as they got their pay, the officers changed into civilian clothes and wore those instead.

207
00:18:40,486 --> 00:18:47,120
When we arrived at the area of military action at Antung airfield, it was the time when the

208
00:18:47,121 --> 00:18:49,380
Americans had superiority in the air.

209
00:18:49,381 --> 00:18:55,920
But as our squadrons appeared there, we took the air supremacy away from them

210
00:18:55,921 --> 00:18:59,720
by attacking their piston fighter planes and bombs.

211
00:19:06,120 --> 00:19:11,460
The sudden appearance of this new Soviet jet fighter would throw the West into a state of shock.

212
00:19:12,360 --> 00:19:16,840
The stage was now set for the first jet versus jet encounters in fighter combat

213
00:19:16,841 --> 00:19:20,180
and a new chapter in the history of air war.

214
00:19:23,620 --> 00:19:28,980
The Korean War was the first war the United States Air Force fought as a separate service.

215
00:19:29,380 --> 00:19:34,140
Before September of 1947, the U.S. Air Force was a division of the U.S. Army.

216
00:19:34,940 --> 00:19:36,520
MiG-15 will be right back.

217
00:19:40,260 --> 00:19:46,020
December 1950, Soviet-built MiG-15s fly into action for the very first time.

218
00:19:46,840 --> 00:19:54,380
The MiG-15 had an enormous impact when it appeared in the skies above Korea.

219
00:19:54,820 --> 00:19:57,500
First, it was a psychological blow to the Americans,

220
00:19:58,080 --> 00:20:04,280
who were shocked, frankly, at being technically outclassed by this new war machine.

221
00:20:05,140 --> 00:20:08,400
And secondly, it had a very severe physical impact.

222
00:20:08,401 --> 00:20:12,720
It was a cut above the propeller-driven aircraft which the Americans were using at the time,

223
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:16,540
and it shot down an awful lot of fighters and particularly bombs.

224
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:26,640
The Russian fighters operated secretly from safe bases around Antong, north of the Yalu River in China.

225
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:34,060
The area between the Yalu River on the Chinese border and central North Korea became known as MiG Alley.

226
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:37,440
Western aircraft entered at their peril.

227
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:46,700
In addition to supplying the Chinese with MiGs, the Russians were also secretly training Chinese and North Korean pilots.

228
00:20:46,840 --> 00:20:58,860
I was one of the 80 chosen to go to Manchuria and train first by the North Koreans to fly the propeller planes.

229
00:20:59,480 --> 00:21:05,160
After that, we turned over to the Soviet Air Force to learn to fly the jet planes.

230
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:09,460
All we learned was just take off and landing. That's about it.

231
00:21:09,461 --> 00:21:14,720
And then they told us that we are now ready for the solo in the MiG-15.

232
00:21:14,721 --> 00:21:23,460
So everybody had some problems because of the tremendous thrust and also high altitude capabilities.

233
00:21:31,860 --> 00:21:36,820
The Koreans, they had to loot from the feudal age into the jet age.

234
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:38,660
Can you imagine what a gap?

235
00:21:38,661 --> 00:21:43,260
They didn't know anything

236
00:21:43,261 --> 00:21:45,500
and they were put into these jet planes.

237
00:21:46,340 --> 00:21:47,560
This wasn't easy to do.

238
00:21:50,080 --> 00:21:52,080
There was a lot of talking and persuading

239
00:21:52,081 --> 00:21:54,300
and we had to convince them of how things had to be done.

240
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:05,140
I needed a whole day to get to know their names

241
00:22:05,141 --> 00:22:06,080
and to get to know them.

242
00:22:06,081 --> 00:22:11,320
I didn't speak any Chinese and those I had to teach didn't know any Russian.

243
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:17,580
The pilots taught them to fly and we taught them how to service and maintain the plane.

244
00:22:20,340 --> 00:22:23,840
But the MiG's brief air supremacy was about to be challenged.

245
00:22:26,080 --> 00:22:33,400
Within weeks, a squadron of the new American F-86 Sabres was rushed from the United States to take the MiGs head on.

246
00:22:36,080 --> 00:22:37,600
You were afraid of them a little bit.

247
00:22:38,100 --> 00:22:41,400
And of course with our F-86s, we knew it was a fine airplane

248
00:22:41,401 --> 00:22:44,480
and we knew it had all kinds of combat capability,

249
00:22:44,660 --> 00:22:48,040
but until you actually engaged the enemy aircraft

250
00:22:48,041 --> 00:22:52,800
and actually found out you against the other fellow how it performed,

251
00:22:53,260 --> 00:22:55,320
then it didn't give you a lot of confidence.

252
00:22:57,340 --> 00:23:01,340
When the MiGs first engaged in head-to-head combat against the Sabres,

253
00:23:01,341 --> 00:23:03,060
they finally met their match.

254
00:23:03,061 --> 00:23:09,580
A new era in aerial combat was about to begin, the jet versus jet dogfight.

255
00:23:09,860 --> 00:23:21,880
We were patrolling on this patrol line when we spotted these four airplanes coming toward us at a very impressive high rate of speed.

256
00:23:22,580 --> 00:23:28,140
My first impression then of the MiG was I was up against something that was really a dynamo.

257
00:23:28,141 --> 00:23:35,720
So I started maneuvering into position to keep hitting him to see if I couldn't get the airplane to blow.

258
00:23:36,800 --> 00:23:44,960
And I used almost my entire load of ammunition on him before he finally looked like he was out of control and went on into the ground.

259
00:23:49,640 --> 00:23:53,520
The MiG pilots realized they no longer had supremacy of the skies.

260
00:23:53,521 --> 00:23:58,980
early dog fights meant that new tactics had to be learned fast

261
00:23:58,981 --> 00:24:10,920
this aerial combat was basically chaotic both vertically and horizontally

262
00:24:10,921 --> 00:24:17,140
but from the battle we learned we should never fight sabers horizontally

263
00:24:17,141 --> 00:24:29,400
The MIG had a better than a 2,500 foot per minute rate of climb advantage.

264
00:24:30,620 --> 00:24:38,020
Could turn tighter, could out-accelerate, could out-decelerate, and we're roughly the same speed.

265
00:24:38,980 --> 00:24:43,760
We talk about the 86 being faster and the MIG being faster.

266
00:24:43,761 --> 00:24:45,560
It depends on what altitude you're at.

267
00:24:45,561 --> 00:24:50,400
Now, we're not talking about a large or an excessive difference.

268
00:24:51,020 --> 00:24:54,120
We're talking maybe three, four, five miles an hour.

269
00:24:57,940 --> 00:25:02,220
The Sabre was a good plane, but its engine seemed to be weaker

270
00:25:02,221 --> 00:25:05,480
because my MIG could climb up and escape from him,

271
00:25:05,820 --> 00:25:08,300
but he could not escape from me.

272
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:12,360
I would always catch up with him.

273
00:25:15,560 --> 00:25:24,020
Our MiG was very well armed, much better than the Sabre.

274
00:25:24,440 --> 00:25:31,500
We had three machine guns, and the caliber of the guns was such that one hit could destroy a Sabre.

275
00:25:40,640 --> 00:25:42,400
We just had .50 caliber.

276
00:25:42,401 --> 00:25:48,760
we could hit the MiG a number of times and the MiG wouldn't necessarily go down

277
00:25:48,761 --> 00:25:56,920
but if the MiG hit an F-86 a few times with those cannons

278
00:25:56,921 --> 00:25:59,320
the F-86 was not going to make it home

279
00:25:59,321 --> 00:26:10,000
we fought against them like sportsmen

280
00:26:10,001 --> 00:26:13,640
whoever handled their plane best won

281
00:26:13,641 --> 00:26:16,140
if you are weak

282
00:26:16,141 --> 00:26:17,960
bad luck

283
00:26:17,961 --> 00:26:27,560
you look for a moment of hesitation

284
00:26:27,561 --> 00:26:29,740
in an air battle

285
00:26:29,741 --> 00:26:32,080
which lasts for say 20 minutes

286
00:26:32,081 --> 00:26:34,580
you may only get 2 or 3 seconds

287
00:26:34,581 --> 00:26:36,940
and once you notice him hesitating

288
00:26:36,941 --> 00:26:39,660
that's when you press the trigger

289
00:26:39,661 --> 00:26:40,840
and hit the target.

290
00:26:44,840 --> 00:26:47,120
The main role of the MiG-15

291
00:26:47,121 --> 00:26:49,260
was to intercept American bombers

292
00:26:49,261 --> 00:26:51,920
raiding North Korea with their saber escorts.

293
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:55,320
The principal MiG tactic

294
00:26:55,321 --> 00:26:57,840
was to take advantage of its superior altitude

295
00:26:57,841 --> 00:27:01,200
and lie in wait at heights up to 50,000 feet

296
00:27:01,201 --> 00:27:04,180
and then ambush the American air armadas below.

297
00:27:05,880 --> 00:27:08,680
If possible, go up to 50,000 feet.

298
00:27:08,681 --> 00:27:11,400
50,000 feet, then American plane wouldn't come up there.

299
00:27:12,060 --> 00:27:16,280
You go up to 50,000 feet and look down at the American plane, go down and shoot.

300
00:27:17,240 --> 00:27:23,840
In October 1951, Lev Ivanov led a Soviet MiG attack on 48 B-29 bombers.

301
00:27:28,660 --> 00:27:33,960
They came flying in from the sea without cover at an angle of 30 degrees towards the bridge,

302
00:27:34,160 --> 00:27:35,720
which was the best approach to bomb it.

303
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:46,440
The Sabres came in to protect the bombers, but they were four minutes late, so naturally we took advantage of that.

304
00:27:51,440 --> 00:27:59,340
As soon as I saw a Sabre, I got onto its tail, aimed, fired, and that was that.

305
00:28:01,820 --> 00:28:06,600
So many bombers were lost that this day became known as Black Tuesday.

306
00:28:07,560 --> 00:28:11,100
The MiGs had outmaneuvered, outflown, and outgunned their opponents.

307
00:28:12,260 --> 00:28:15,660
With precious few sabers available to stand up to the MiGs,

308
00:28:16,100 --> 00:28:19,000
it looked as though the challenge of the saber was overrated.

309
00:28:19,620 --> 00:28:23,100
But for the Russians, the price of keeping their activities secret

310
00:28:23,101 --> 00:28:26,840
would play a major role in the battle for the skies over Korea.

311
00:28:31,160 --> 00:28:36,540
On December 17, 1950, in the first known combat between swept-wing fighters,

312
00:28:36,600 --> 00:28:43,180
Lieutenant Colonel Bruce Hinton became the first F-86 Sabre fighter pilot to shoot down a MiG-15.

313
00:28:43,840 --> 00:28:45,580
Modern Marvels will be right back.

314
00:28:47,060 --> 00:28:51,660
The Russian MiGs that roamed the skies above Korea had astonished the West.

315
00:28:53,940 --> 00:28:56,840
Now total victory for either side looked unlikely.

316
00:28:58,920 --> 00:29:03,080
The Korean War would be a limited war for the West to contain communism

317
00:29:03,081 --> 00:29:07,160
and for the communists to avoid the humiliation of defeat.

318
00:29:09,500 --> 00:29:12,540
The Soviet MiG-15s enjoyed many advantages.

319
00:29:13,360 --> 00:29:16,000
They were flown out of bases across the border in China,

320
00:29:16,280 --> 00:29:18,540
which were out of bounds to the American air crews.

321
00:29:19,180 --> 00:29:23,420
So after combat in MiG Alley, the MiGs had a safe haven to return to.

322
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:30,320
Although the MiGs were flying in Chinese colors,

323
00:29:30,321 --> 00:29:36,520
It was an open secret that many of the pilots were actually highly experienced Russian aces from World War II.

324
00:29:38,920 --> 00:29:42,160
But Russian pilots were walking a deadly fine line.

325
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:44,763
Neither side could admit that U.S.

326
00:29:44,764 --> 00:29:48,100
and Soviet jet pilots were facing each other in open combat

327
00:29:48,101 --> 00:29:51,460
for fear of escalating the conflict into a nuclear war.

328
00:29:51,461 --> 00:30:01,240
the Russians, the Soviets were very hesitant for obvious reasons to say they were flying the big 15 aircraft

329
00:30:01,241 --> 00:30:08,640
and so they simply never claimed or admitted that they were flying the airplanes but we knew

330
00:30:08,641 --> 00:30:13,460
I mean we could listen to the broadcasts of fighter controllers and the fighter pilots

331
00:30:13,461 --> 00:30:15,320
and it was clearly Russians flying the airplanes

332
00:30:15,321 --> 00:30:28,260
we were told to only communicate in Chinese during a combat

333
00:30:28,261 --> 00:30:32,300
but it didn't work because we always broke into Russian

334
00:30:32,301 --> 00:30:39,220
and sometimes we even broke the bounds of decent language

335
00:30:39,221 --> 00:30:42,100
and we would swear at each other in the air

336
00:30:42,101 --> 00:30:48,300
The Soviet pilots experienced other frustrations as well.

337
00:30:48,840 --> 00:30:51,260
They were not allowed to fly over enemy territory,

338
00:30:51,680 --> 00:30:55,940
in case they were shot down and a Russian pilot was captured and paraded before the world.

339
00:30:57,560 --> 00:31:03,420
Nor could they fly over the sea for fear of being shot down and both aircraft and pilot being captured.

340
00:31:03,421 --> 00:31:10,420
Well, personally, I was able to fly to my plane.

341
00:31:10,421 --> 00:31:17,240
I decided that I must not be taken prisoner, and that I would rather crash the plane and kill myself.

342
00:31:18,460 --> 00:31:20,940
I would not become a prisoner of this.

343
00:31:31,940 --> 00:31:36,060
One pilot, who had to be ejected, crossed the front line by mistake.

344
00:31:37,120 --> 00:31:38,860
He was encircled by enemy troops.

345
00:31:38,861 --> 00:31:47,460
So rather than being taken prisoner and being tortured and humiliated, he took his own life by shooting himself.

346
00:31:48,840 --> 00:31:50,900
I think I would have acted in the same way.

347
00:31:55,540 --> 00:32:02,080
The war for supremacy in the air began to produce aces on both sides.

348
00:32:02,081 --> 00:32:06,820
Colonel Yevgeny Pepalayev had the highest number of kills per mission

349
00:32:06,821 --> 00:32:10,500
shooting down an enemy aircraft once every five missions.

350
00:32:16,500 --> 00:32:28,820
Out of 20 planes, I shot down 16 sabers, 2 F-94s, 1 F-98 and 1 F-84.

351
00:32:28,821 --> 00:32:38,280
To shoot down an enemy plane, we tried to approach him from behind and as close as we could to his tail.

352
00:32:40,520 --> 00:32:42,880
The closer you are, the more likely to hit.

353
00:32:48,560 --> 00:32:54,340
I had the gun sight almost on him, but it wasn't there and I didn't fire.

354
00:32:55,440 --> 00:32:57,480
You don't shoot until the picture is right.

355
00:32:57,481 --> 00:33:01,988
He did not get away. I knocked his engine out.

356
00:33:01,989 --> 00:33:06,300
He was almost being consumed by engine fire.

357
00:33:09,820 --> 00:33:17,200
During these dramatic dogfights in the air, MiG pilots discovered the remarkable abilities of their aircraft to take enormous punishment.

358
00:33:17,201 --> 00:33:30,900
I was flying towards the ground following my leader as it was my responsibility to guard his tail.

359
00:33:32,200 --> 00:33:35,480
The American plane was waiting there for me to come out of the dive.

360
00:33:36,200 --> 00:33:37,660
It was then that he shocked me.

361
00:33:47,200 --> 00:34:00,400
Then during the dive, I realized I must save myself, so I made a decision to eject.

362
00:34:01,200 --> 00:34:04,760
I moved my hand to the ejection handle and thought I'd pressed it,

363
00:34:04,761 --> 00:34:11,600
because in that moment I felt such G-force, such an enormous amount, I felt my bones were cracking.

364
00:34:12,480 --> 00:34:15,980
Then everything went dark and I fainted.

365
00:34:17,200 --> 00:34:31,200
When I came to, I opened my eyes and saw I was still in the plane.

366
00:34:32,560 --> 00:34:33,500
This came as a shock.

367
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:37,500
If I had ejected myself, why was I still inside the plane?

368
00:34:41,600 --> 00:34:43,840
I grabbed the stick to gain control.

369
00:34:44,700 --> 00:34:48,300
And at that moment I heard a horrendous mechanical screeching.

370
00:34:49,060 --> 00:34:51,060
My brain was in overdrive.

371
00:34:51,900 --> 00:34:53,060
What shall I do next?

372
00:34:54,000 --> 00:34:56,500
As all the hydraulic systems had been blown apart,

373
00:34:57,040 --> 00:35:00,120
Bondarenko did not know whether his landing gear was operational.

374
00:35:07,200 --> 00:35:11,600
I thought that I'd have to do a belly landing with just the left wheel out.

375
00:35:11,601 --> 00:35:15,480
And as I approached the ground, I hit it with the wheel.

376
00:35:16,280 --> 00:35:19,360
And luckily for me, then the whole undercarriage came down.

377
00:35:23,900 --> 00:35:28,860
I was so happy that this plane had saved my life.

378
00:35:29,500 --> 00:35:31,300
It's not just words, it's true.

379
00:35:31,960 --> 00:35:35,680
It used all its remaining strength to return me home and land.

380
00:35:35,681 --> 00:35:43,960
I came up to the plane and I kissed it on the nose

381
00:35:43,961 --> 00:35:47,880
and I thought, how wonderful we have such miraculous technology.

382
00:35:56,280 --> 00:35:59,800
The MiG-15 had earned its affectionate nickname,

383
00:36:00,260 --> 00:36:02,920
Somal Yacht Soldat, the soldier aircraft.

384
00:36:05,680 --> 00:36:11,460
By spring 1953, the Korean Air War was at a stalemate.

385
00:36:13,160 --> 00:36:16,460
The U.S. military, keen to capture the Russian technology,

386
00:36:17,620 --> 00:36:23,080
offered a huge reward and political asylum to any pilot that delivered a MiG to South Korea.

387
00:36:23,700 --> 00:36:25,160
No one had yet responded.

388
00:36:26,060 --> 00:36:33,460
But a daring escape attempt by a lone pilot would provide a last-minute twist to a war that seemed to have no end.

389
00:36:33,461 --> 00:36:44,640
On December 22, 1950, F-86 Sabres shot down six MiG-15s over North Korea during the largest air battle of the Korean War.

390
00:36:45,180 --> 00:36:46,520
Modern Marvels will be right back.

391
00:36:48,060 --> 00:36:53,760
By 1953, both sides recognized that the Korean War was unwinnable.

392
00:36:54,160 --> 00:37:00,320
After two years of negotiations, in July, a truce was finally signed at Panmunjom.

393
00:37:00,321 --> 00:37:04,320
but the Korean Peninsula would remain a divided armed camp.

394
00:37:06,320 --> 00:37:10,080
The fighting in Korea came to an end in July 1953

395
00:37:10,081 --> 00:37:12,880
but of course the Cold War still went on

396
00:37:12,881 --> 00:37:17,240
and during it both sides were trying to get hold of one another's secrets

397
00:37:17,241 --> 00:37:19,720
particularly in terms of military technology.

398
00:37:20,860 --> 00:37:25,860
The Americans were especially anxious to get their hands on a MiG-15.

399
00:37:25,861 --> 00:37:31,040
The military made an offer of $100,000

400
00:37:31,041 --> 00:37:34,020
equivalent to several million dollars today

401
00:37:34,021 --> 00:37:38,980
and political asylum for the first pilot who would defect and deliver a MiG intact

402
00:37:38,981 --> 00:37:44,320
The 21-year-old North Korean pilot, Lieutenant Noh Koon-Suk

403
00:37:44,321 --> 00:37:46,080
an ardent anti-communist

404
00:37:46,081 --> 00:37:50,300
was already planning one of the most spectacular escapes from the communist bloc

405
00:37:50,301 --> 00:37:55,000
So my aim was how to defect successfully

406
00:37:55,001 --> 00:38:00,480
and land on American Air Base so that I can ask for political asylum.

407
00:38:00,840 --> 00:38:05,080
So on September 21st, Sunday morning, even before breakfast,

408
00:38:06,020 --> 00:38:09,100
I got in the plane and took off to the north.

409
00:38:09,640 --> 00:38:15,300
And then the plane went to the north and I came down south and toward Pyongyang, toward the east.

410
00:38:15,960 --> 00:38:18,760
And that's where I finally made the defect.

411
00:38:18,761 --> 00:38:20,040
It was not easy.

412
00:38:20,041 --> 00:38:26,960
In fact, that defection was harder than equivalent to, say, 50 combat missions.

413
00:38:28,100 --> 00:38:32,440
Now, south of the demilitarized zone, he flashed into UN territory.

414
00:38:33,700 --> 00:38:37,815
Just four tense minutes after crossing the border, he spotted the U.S.

415
00:38:37,816 --> 00:38:40,460
Kimpo Air Base and decided to risk a landing.

416
00:38:41,640 --> 00:38:47,300
Radar people missed me, and also anti-air gunners I was afraid of. They missed me.

417
00:38:48,060 --> 00:38:54,200
He decided to land when, to his amazement, he saw a saber coming straight towards him at the opposite end of the runway.

418
00:38:56,280 --> 00:39:01,160
Well, he hadn't seen me, so we landed together, both ends.

419
00:39:01,940 --> 00:39:06,760
Then we passed in the runway, and of course I knew he was there, he was coming down.

420
00:39:07,020 --> 00:39:09,060
But he hadn't seen me until we passed.

421
00:39:10,260 --> 00:39:12,380
After passed, of course, he was frightened.

422
00:39:12,381 --> 00:39:17,380
that at the end of the runway, he stopped the engine and he jumped out.

423
00:39:18,040 --> 00:39:21,680
And he was wondering what kind of nuts landed from the wrong side.

424
00:39:22,940 --> 00:39:26,520
Commanders at the base panicked, thinking the war had started again

425
00:39:26,521 --> 00:39:28,560
and scrambled every plane they had.

426
00:39:34,060 --> 00:39:37,180
Lieutenant Noh Koon Sok did not know of the reward.

427
00:39:37,620 --> 00:39:39,660
He just wanted to fly to freedom.

428
00:39:42,380 --> 00:39:49,760
The surrendered MiG-15 was taken to Okinawa, where it was dismantled and rigorously examined.

429
00:39:51,500 --> 00:39:56,560
It was later airlifted to the United States and underwent further exhaustive flight testing.

430
00:39:57,520 --> 00:40:04,700
The plane was pushed to its limits by America's top test pilots, Tom Collins and the world-renowned Chuck Yeager.

431
00:40:04,701 --> 00:40:10,900
I told Chuck Yeager that don't deliberately go into spinning stall

432
00:40:10,901 --> 00:40:13,840
because this plane is very quirky.

433
00:40:14,040 --> 00:40:16,260
Sometimes you get out, sometimes you cannot come out.

434
00:40:16,660 --> 00:40:19,020
If you cannot come out, you have to bail out and you lose the plane.

435
00:40:19,440 --> 00:40:23,240
So he didn't put the plane into deliberate stall.

436
00:40:24,100 --> 00:40:27,720
Americans wondered what a MiG-15 could be a supersonic.

437
00:40:28,520 --> 00:40:31,060
Chuck Yeager has proved that it's not a supersonic

438
00:40:31,061 --> 00:40:33,920
because he actually went up to 50,000 feet.

439
00:40:33,921 --> 00:40:41,200
He died straight down, and he almost lost his life because he was totally out of control.

440
00:40:41,360 --> 00:40:47,840
The control system didn't respond until he got down as low as near 3,000 feet.

441
00:40:48,460 --> 00:40:55,860
And the chaser plane flown by Tom Collins, hand-gestured him to bail out.

442
00:40:56,480 --> 00:41:01,200
But he didn't bail out, and somehow he pulled out at 3,000 feet above the water.

443
00:41:01,201 --> 00:41:09,080
The test flights proved that indeed the MiG was an exceptional plane, but revealed no new supersonic technology.

444
00:41:09,940 --> 00:41:13,394
The defector, Lieutenant Noh Koon-Sok, went to work for the U.S.

445
00:41:13,395 --> 00:41:15,660
government and became an American citizen.

446
00:41:17,000 --> 00:41:21,420
With the Korean truce signed, the Soviet MiG pilots returned home.

447
00:41:21,780 --> 00:41:25,200
But the MiG-15 had already earned its place in history.

448
00:41:26,320 --> 00:41:31,080
Eventually, the Russians claimed the MiG combat record gave them a 4-to-1 advantage.

449
00:41:31,200 --> 00:41:38,040
The U.S. claimed a 10 to 1 ratio in favor of the F-86 Sabre against the MiG-15.

450
00:41:44,880 --> 00:41:52,440
But whatever the final score, the MiG-15 was an extraordinary aeronautical achievement and way ahead of its time.

451
00:41:52,441 --> 00:42:02,240
I think in terms of the challenges that the Soviet design bureaus confronted in putting together the MiG-15,

452
00:42:02,840 --> 00:42:07,560
given the limitations of technology that they confronted in industrial production,

453
00:42:07,561 --> 00:42:14,720
that they did an incredible job in putting together an aircraft that could really challenge the F-86.

454
00:42:14,721 --> 00:42:24,320
The plane was very easy to handle and very reliable.

455
00:42:25,760 --> 00:42:31,160
At high altitude, it was superior to the Sabre because of its ability to operate at a much higher ceiling.

456
00:42:35,280 --> 00:42:40,240
